## Source Code Layout & Organization

### Indentation: spaces, not tabs
- Use **spaces** for indentation. No hard tabs.

### 2-space body indent
Use **2 spaces** to indent the bodies of forms that have body parameters. This covers all `def` forms, special forms, and macros that introduce local bindings (e.g. `loop`, `let`, `when-let`) and many macros like `when`, `cond`, `as->`, `cond->`, `case`, `with-*`, etc.

✅ Good:

```clojure
(when something
  (something-else))

(with-out-str
  (println "Hello, ")
  (println "world!"))
```

❌ Bad — four spaces:

```clojure
(when something
    (something-else))
```

❌ Bad — one space:

```clojure
(with-out-str
 (println "Hello, ")
 (println "world!"))
```

### Vertically align fn/macro arguments spanning multiple lines

✅ Good:

```clojure
(filter even?
        (range 1 10))
```

❌ Bad:

```clojure
(filter even?
  (range 1 10))
```

### Single-space indent when no args are on the fn-name line

When a function/macro call places **no** arguments on the same line as the function name, use **single-space indent** from the opening paren.

✅ Good:

```clojure
(filter
 even?
 (range 1 10))

(or
 ala
 bala
 portokala)
```

❌ Bad — two spaces:

```clojure
(filter
   even?
   (range 1 10))

(or
  ala
  bala
  portokala)
```

### Vertically align `let` bindings and map keys

✅ Good:

```clojure
(let [thing1 "some stuff"
      thing2 "other stuff"]
  {:thing1 thing1
   :thing2 thing2})
```

❌ Bad:

```clojure
(let [thing1 "some stuff"
  thing2 "other stuff"]
  {:thing1 thing1
   :thing2 thing2})
```

